  "body": "# Agents in worlds (MCP bridge) — v1 — first-iteration design + spike plan\n\nPart of the Phase 14 core topic #6280 (Agents in worlds (MCP bridge)), filed from the next-horizons discussion #1240 per Drew's 2026-11-05 decision. The Phase 14 epic links the whole map.\n\n## Scope (rough outline — the core topic's research refines it)\nMCP bridge spike: one agent joins a world as a client, reads screens, posts chat, performs one bounded program interaction — all under a capability ceiling with a full audit log. Deliverable: spike plan + design doc for agent identity/roles/guardrails + breakdown.\n\n## Entry condition\nThe core topic's research (#6280) has produced its first-pass findings, and the design-queue items ahead of it (see core issue) are moving.\n\n## Deliverable\nA design document (in `.opencode/plans/` or `doc/`) plus a breakdown of implementation issues for this step, linked from this issue and the core topic.\n\nDrew's Phase 14 principles (#1240) govern this step: local-first always; hostable by anyone at any scale; p2p behavior identical to hosted behavior; our hosted instance is the public backstop, never a requirement.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- Entry condition verified and recorded on this issue: the core topic research (#6280) has produced first-pass findings, and the design-queue items ahead of this step are moving.\n- A design document exists in `.opencode/plans/` or `doc/` covering: one agent joining a world as an MCP-bridge client, reading screens, posting chat, and performing one bounded program interaction — all under a capability ceiling with a full audit log — plus agent identity, roles, and guardrails, consistent with the Phase 14 principles from #1240 (local-first; hostable by anyone; p2p behavior identical to hosted; hosted instance is a backstop, never a requirement).\n- An implementation breakdown (linked issues) for the spike is filed and linked from this issue and the core topic #6280, with native sub-issue/dependency relations where the platform supports them.\n",
